<h2>Which platform is best for lead generation?</h2>
<p>Google Ads is often a practical starting point when customers actively search for the service. Meta Ads can work well when the offer is visually understandable, the audience can be reached through creative and follow-up is strong. ChatGPT Ads may be useful when the offer fits research and comparison conversations.</p>
<p>No platform fixes a weak offer, generic landing page or slow sales response. Compare qualified leads and customers, not just the cost of a click.</p>

<h2>How measurement differs</h2>
<p>All three platforms can report delivery and engagement metrics, but attribution is never the same as business truth. Use consistent UTMs, conversion definitions and CRM feedback wherever possible. Separate raw leads from qualified leads and closed customers.</p>
<p>ChatGPT Ads Manager currently reports impressions, clicks, spend, CTR, average CPC, average CPM and configured conversions. Because the product is in beta and broad benchmarks remain limited, first-party measurement is especially important.</p>

<h2>Cost comparison: avoid the wrong question</h2>
<p>It is too early to say one channel is universally cheaper. A more useful comparison holds the business goal constant. If the same spend generates more qualified customers at an acceptable CAC, that channel is doing the better job for that offer during that period.</p>
<p>Do not compare a Meta awareness campaign with a Google search campaign only by CPC. They are performing different jobs. Compare reach and lift for awareness, qualified enquiries for lead generation, and revenue or margin for acquisition.</p>

<h2>When to choose each platform</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Choose ChatGPT Ads</strong> for a controlled experiment around research, comparison or decision-stage conversations.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose Google Ads</strong> when high-intent search demand already exists and the business has a relevant landing page.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose Meta Ads</strong> when visual creative and audience discovery can create interest or support retargeting.</li>
    <li><strong>Use a mix</strong> when each channel has a defined role and measurement does not double-count the same outcome.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A practical channel test</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Use the same clear offer and qualification rules.</li>
    <li>Build channel-native creative rather than copying one ad everywhere.</li>
    <li>Send traffic to relevant, trackable landing pages.</li>
    <li>Run long enough to gather usable signals without exceeding the loss limit.</li>
    <li>Compare qualified CPL, CAC, customer value and sales feedback.</li>
</ol>

